summary: How this site went from Markdown glue to one Go binary with a Neorg parser I wrote myself, and what I would keep.
tags: [go, neorg, web]
draft: false
@end

The old version of this site was a pile of Markdown files glued together with a templating helper. It worked until I wanted project devlogs, tags, and search. Each one meant more glue, so in April I started over.

* What I wanted
- Write in `.norg` and publish the file as it is.
- Ship one Go binary: no database, no Node at runtime.
- Give each project a folder, with its devlogs next to it.

* What runs the site today
@table
| Layer | Choice |
| - | - |
| HTTP | Go `net/http` and its stdlib router |
| Templates | templ, compiled to Go |
| Content | a Neorg parser I wrote, about 1,000 lines |
| Code blocks | Chroma for highlighting |
| Styles | Tailwind v3 standalone binary plus handwritten CSS |
| Logs | zerolog |
| Deploy | a distroless Docker image on Railway |
@end

The app parses every `.norg` file once at startup and keeps the results in memory. A request reads from a slice and renders a template. I have no cache to invalidate, because nothing changes until the next deploy.

All of it comes to about 2,400 lines of Go, with roughly 2,200 more lines of tests.

* How it came together
** April: an empty shell
My first commit fixed a `go:embed` build that broke when the CSS file did not exist yet. A humble start.

** May: the parser and the projects
The Neorg parser landed on May 2. A week later it handled tables, task states, definition lists, spoilers, and inline math. On May 19 projects became folders: `index.norg` describes the project, and every other file in the folder turns into a devlog at `/projects/{slug}/{post}`.

** July: hardening and a visual pass
I ran `govulncheck`, and it flagged a reachable vulnerability in the `crypto/tls` package of Go 1.26.4. I moved to 1.26.5 and kept pulling the thread:
- The parser validates every link it renders, since its HTML goes out through `templ.Raw`.
- Search answers `400` to any query over 256 bytes.
- The build pins the Tailwind download by SHA-256 and pins GitHub Actions to commits.

The next day I self-hosted Source Serif 4 and Source Sans 3 and moved the palette to warm paper and espresso. Two weeks after that, I split the parser out of a single 1,015-line file into its own package of five files.

** October: a front door
Until this week, `/` redirected to `/blog`. Now it opens on a menu with three doors: Blog, Projects, and About. Each door shows a photo from my coffee routine: writing over a cup, a pour-over at home, a takeaway latte from the café. Hover one and its frame grows from 96 to 192 pixels, a trick I took from [Fernando Borretti's site](https://borretti.me). Click it and a CSS view transition slides the site name into the header of the next page, with no JavaScript.

* What I would keep
Parsing at startup was the best call. A broken `.norg` file stops the app at boot, where I see it, instead of failing in front of a reader.

Writing the parser myself comes second. It took longer than pulling in a Markdown library, but I can read every line that turns my notes into HTML. That made the security pass a short afternoon instead of an audit of someone else's renderer.

* What is still rough
Search scores each word on its own. A search for `bento grid` returns anything that mentions /bento/ or /grid/, and /rebuild/ does not match /rebuilt/. Phrase matching and stemming come next.

The devlogs on the [Blog Engine project page](/projects/blog-engine) have the details.
